<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h2>
<p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Supporting Our Community</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Volunteering in Our Community</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Interpret dialogues from various social contexts. Interactive: Highlight opinions, both positive and negative, related to an audio or video recording, during discussions.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can interpret perspectives in dialogues on volunteer work (e.g., "In Panama, volunteer programs such as disaster relief initiatives help during emergencies, but some believe the government should allocate more resources for long-term infrastructure.")<br>Can highlight positive and negative opinions in audio recordings about community support (e.g., "Some community members believe we should increase support for Basura Cero initiatives, while others argue that more focus is needed on improving public transportation.")<br>Can ask questions to clarify viewpoints in community discussions (e.g., "What have the local authorities been doing to improve services for residents?" "The local authorities have been expanding public transportation.")</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images showing various community volunteer activities in Panama (e.g., cleaning up a local park, assisting at a community shelter, distributing resources after a natural disaster). Students work in pairs to briefly describe what they see and share if they have ever participated in or witnessed similar activities. The teacher facilitates a quick whole-class share-out, prompting students with questions like: "What kind of support is being provided here?" or "Why do people dedicate their time to these projects?"</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces 8-10 key vocabulary words relevant to the theme using pictures and brief definitions. Words introduced include: <i>volunteers, community, programs, resources, allocate, improve, underserved, contribute, initiative, impact</i>. The teacher models the pronunciation of each word, specifically focusing on stress patterns. For example, for "Community programs are funded by the government," the teacher explicitly models: "Com-MU-ni-ty PRO-grams are FUN-ded by the GOV-ern-ment," highlighting stressed syllables. The teacher also uses the passive voice to describe simple community actions (e.g., "Trash is collected by volunteers.") and modals for necessity ("Individuals should contribute to recycling efforts.").</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the newly introduced vocabulary and concepts.
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a community is 'underserved', does it have all the necessary support, or does it need more?" (Expected answer: It needs more support.)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When the government 'allocates resources', does it hide them or distribute them for specific use?" (Expected answer: Distributes them.)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a 'volunteer initiative' something people are paid to do?" (Expected answer: No, they dedicate their time.)</li>
        </ul></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 minutes]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Supporting Our Community."</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students will listen to an audio recording of a dialogue between two Panamanian community leaders, Maria and Ricardo, discussing current volunteer projects and community needs in their neighborhood. The dialogue, approximately 2 minutes long, will naturally embed the target vocabulary (e.g., <i>government, programs, resources, services, volunteers, projects, influence, decision-making, underserved, improve, contribute, promote, initiatives</i>) and grammatical structures (passive voice, modals of necessity, present perfect continuous). For instance, Maria might say, "The 'Basura Cero' initiative has been having a positive 'impact', but some areas are still 'underserved'," and Ricardo could reply, "Yes, resources 'should be allocated' more effectively, and local 'programs are funded' by both the 'government' and private groups."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Receptive: Interpret dialogues from various social contexts. Interactive: Highlight opinions, both positive and negative, related to an audio or video recording, during discussions.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Listening (Gist):</b> Students listen once to grasp the main idea. Teacher asks: "What are Maria and Ricardo primarily discussing?" (Expected answer: Various volunteer projects and challenges in their community.) "What is their overall sentiment about community support?" (Expected answer: They are discussing both progress and areas needing improvement.)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Listening (Specific Details):</b> Students listen again and complete a True/False activity based on specific statements about the dialogue. For example:
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"True or False: The 'Basura Cero' initiative has been improving public services."</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"True or False: Ricardo believes the government should allocate all resources for volunteer projects."</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"True or False: Volunteers have been working to address the needs of underserved areas."</li>
            </ul></li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third Listening (Opinions):</b> Students listen one more time to identify specific positive and negative opinions expressed by Maria and Ricardo. They jot down notes for each speaker under "Positive Opinions" and "Negative Opinions."</li>
        </ul></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10-12 minutes]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice listening for specific vocabulary and grammatical structures (passive voice, modals for necessity, present perfect continuous) within the context of interpreting opinions and details about community support.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Gap Fill:</b> The teacher plays selected sentences from the dialogue again, pausing after each one. Students complete a handout with sentences containing gaps for target vocabulary words or grammatical structures. For example: "Community _____ are funded by the government and private donations." (programs) "Individuals _____ contribute to community projects for a greater impact." (should/must) "Volunteers have been _____ tirelessly to improve public services." (working). This activity reinforces accurate listening for specific words and structures.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - Sentence Reordering and Matching:</b> Students are given jumbled sentence parts from the dialogue that feature passive voice or present perfect continuous structures. They must listen to the original sentence and reorder it correctly. Alternatively, they might match sentence halves, one part containing a modal verb and the other explaining its necessity (e.g., "We should allocate resources" matched with "to support underserved communities"). This helps them internalize the sentence structure and word order while listening for confirmation.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - Opinion Matching:</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher plays short excerpts from the dialogue that contain either a positive or negative opinion. After each excerpt, students quickly discuss and agree on whether the opinion expressed is positive or negative, and identify keywords that indicate this. They then write down one sentence summarizing the opinion and its polarity (e.g., "Maria expressed a positive opinion about the increased 'involvement' of youth in projects."). This practices highlighting opinions through collaborative listening and peer checking.</li>
        </ul></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[15-17 minutes]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Supporting Our Community."</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Small Group Discussion and Opinion Synthesis.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups of 3-4. Each group receives a brief audio clip (30-45 seconds) presenting a specific community challenge and two differing opinions about how to address it. For example, one clip might discuss "lack of funding for local sports programs," with one speaker suggesting increased government allocation and another proposing community-based fundraising initiatives. Another clip might address "poor public transportation in underserved areas," with one opinion advocating for more routes and another for improved infrastructure.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to their assigned audio clip twice. Their task is to:
            <ol>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identify the main community challenge.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Highlight the positive and negative opinions/suggestions presented by the speakers.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engage in a discussion within their group, using phrases like: "Speaker A mentioned that...", "I think that's a positive approach because...", "However, Speaker B believes that...", "What have the local authorities been doing to improve services for residents in this area?" (Pragmatic and Sociolinguistic focus: engaging in discussions about civic responsibilities, asking clarifying questions, leading collaborative discussions).</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Using the target grammar (passive voice, modals, present perfect continuous) and vocabulary, they must collaboratively formulate two key opinions (one positive, one negative) and a clarifying question for each perspective. For instance, "Community programs for sports 'should be funded' more, but 'resources are currently being allocated' to other areas." "What has the local 'government been doing' to 'promote' youth 'involvement' in sports?"</li>
            </ol></li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Finally, each group briefly reports their synthesized opinions and questions to the whole class, explaining what they listened for and how they interpreted the different perspectives.</li>
        </ul></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10-12 minutes]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist during the group discussion and report-out, focusing on each student's ability to: (a) actively listen and interpret opinions, (b) use target vocabulary, (c) correctly apply passive voice, modals, and present perfect continuous, and (d) ask clarifying questions.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in a brief whole-class Q&A session where the teacher poses questions about the different group reports. Students respond, demonstrating their ability to interpret diverse viewpoints and use the target language. For example, the teacher might ask, "Based on Group 3's report, what negative opinion was highlighted regarding public transportation? And what question did they raise about the government's efforts?"</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Students correctly stress content words and maintain rhythm when speaking (e.g., Com-MU-ni-ty PRO-grams).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Accurate and appropriate use of at least 5-7 new vocabulary words (e.g., <i>underserved, allocate, influence, initiative, contribute</i>).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity in interpreting opinions:</b> Ability to clearly distinguish and articulate positive and negative opinions from the audio.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Ability to ask clarifying questions:</b> Formulating grammatically correct and relevant questions to seek further information or understanding of viewpoints.</li>
        </ul></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket with the following prompts:
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one positive opinion you heard today about community support that resonated with you?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging for you when trying to interpret different perspectives in the audio recordings?"</li>
        </ul></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5 minutes]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection, connection to next lesson.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now in your ability to understand and highlight different opinions in English dialogues about community issues?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which of the new vocabulary words or grammatical structures from today's lesson do you think will be most useful for you in future discussions about civic responsibilities?"</li>
        </ul></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> Today's listening activities were effective in introducing key vocabulary and grammar. Students engaged well in identifying gist and specific details from the initial audio. The opinion-highlighting activity in pairs and groups proved slightly challenging for some, requiring careful re-listening and peer negotiation. More explicit modeling of "asking questions to clarify viewpoints" could be beneficial next time. The pronunciation focus on stressed syllables was integrated, but continuous reinforcement will be needed. The B1.3 level tasks required active critical listening and collaborative discussion, which was largely achieved. The scenario "Supporting Our Community" resonated well with students' prior knowledge.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson (Lesson 2):</b> In the next lesson, we will move to reading comprehension. Students will read an article about successful volunteer initiatives in various Panamanian communities, reinforcing the vocabulary, grammar (passive voice, modals, present perfect continuous), and concepts of community support and civic responsibilities introduced in this listening lesson.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[3 minutes]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>